    "product_id": "48b1bdf9-7015-4bd7-a687-2fc268db16ab",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 161728\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1028 AM PDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Gusty north to northeast winds this morning will diminish through \n  the afternoon.\n\n* Precipitation chances return to the region early to middle of\n  next week, with significant uncertainty in forecast and impact\n  details.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through the Middle of Next Week.\n\nThrough the overnight, gusty northeast winds were reports across \nportions of the area at times. The strongest winds were in the \nterrain with gusts over 40 MPH reported as higher elevations \nintercepted a 50KT+ 850-700mb jet. This lower level jet associated \nwith a low pressure system sitting over southern California will \nremain draped over southern portions of the region through rest of \ntonight before slowly shifting south and weakening this morning. \nWhile gusts are fairly isolated at the moment, expecting a quick \nuptick in the valleys early this morning as better mixing occurs \nwith sunrise as well as an area of better vorticity advection on the \nbackside of the low. A Wind Advisory remains in effect through 10AM \nthis morning which looks good as additional minor wind impacts are \npossible with the uptick in winds after sunrise. Winds will diminish \nthis afternoon as the low shifts south and weakens. Widespread gusts \n20-30 MPH are expected this afternoon, then gusts under 20 MPH are \nexpected through at least Monday. After a brief warm up to near \nnormal today, temperatures drop a few degrees back to slightly below \nnormal levels for the weekend. \n\nContinuing to watch the potential for weather impacts around midweek \nas a deep West Coast trough impacts the region. Strong forcing will \nbe associated with this deepening low, which some models have \nclosing off as it moves into central or southern California. This \nforcing will interact with an uptick in moisture that advects into \nthe region from distant Tropical Storm Simon ahead of the trough. \nCluster analysis shows significant variation in how quickly the \ntrough moves into the region as well as how deep it will be once it \ndoes so. In addition, models continue to advertise differing \nsolutions on how an IVT plume sets up somewhere over southern Nevada \nor western Arizona. All that to say- there is significant \nuncertainty in the forecast details and potential impacts the middle \nof next week. It's fair to say precipitation is expected at some \npoint after Monday, with snow possible above 8000-9000ft, but rain \nimpacts will not be as significant as what just occurred with the \nremnants of Hurricane Rachel. There also should be an increase in \nwinds ahead of the trough, likely Tuesday, which could result in \nwind impacts. Low confidence though in exact timing of rain, where \nthe highest impacts will occur, and how much snow to expect in the \nhigher elevations.With the incoming system and the potential for \nrain, temperatures will cool and remain below normal early next week \nwhen the system moves inland.\n\n&&\n\n \n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package... \nNortheast winds will continue through the afternoon, with occasional \ngusts of 25 to 30 knots persisting through sunset. Winds will fall \nbelow 12 knots overnight, with directions becoming more variable but \nstill maintaining a northerly component. Northeast breezes return \nSaturday, although speeds will be lighter than today and are \nexpected to remain below 10 knots. VFR conditions will prevail \nthrough Saturday, with mainly clear skies through tonight, followed \nby increasing mid- and high-level clouds tomorrow morning.  \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Gusty winds will \ncontinue through the afternoon, with gusts generally 15 to 30 knots \nacross much of the region. Across the southern Great Basin and \nwestern Mojave Desert, wind directions will favor northeast to east \ndirections, while northerly winds are expected in the Colorado River \nValley and northern Owens Valley. Winds will diminish overnight, \nwith most areas seeing speeds fall below 10 knots. VFR conditions \nwill prevail, with FEW to SCT high clouds spreading from south to \nnorth overnight and Saturday morning. \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Nickerson\nAVIATION...Planz\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
